will-server for Debian
----------------------

The server listens on TCP port 7770 by default (override with --port).

After installation the systemd unit `will-server.service` is enabled.
Logs: journalctl -u will-server -f

The daemon runs as the unprivileged system user `will-server`.
Message history is stored in /var/lib/will-server/will.db (set by will-server.service).

OTP authentication
------------------

On install, postinst generates a random OTP hash salt in
/etc/will-server/environment (WILL_OTP_HASH_SALT). The systemd unit starts the
server via /usr/lib/will-server/will-server-start, which passes the salt and
enables --log-otp-for-dev.

OTP codes are written to the journal (not sent via SMS):

  journalctl -u will-server -f

Look for lines like: [dev] OTP for +15551234567: 123456

This is intended for staging and development deployments. For production with
real users, disable --log-otp-for-dev and integrate a real SMS provider.

Scaling (single process)
------------------------

will-server uses an asynchronous I/O model (standalone Asio) suitable for
roughly 2k-5k concurrent connections on one host, depending on CPU, RAM, and
message rate.

Recommended production settings:

- systemd LimitNOFILE=65535 (already set in will-server.service)
- Ensure the process ulimits allow open files: ulimit -n
- Optional CLI flags:
    --max-clients 4096
    --listen-backlog 1024
    --io-threads 4

Load testing:

  will-server --port 7770 &
  will-load-clients --host 127.0.0.1 --port 7770 --clients 2000 --hold-seconds 10

Acceptance: 2000 idle connections with failures=0 in load_clients output.
